-- P5 (docs/EARTH-TILES-PLAN.md): FREEZE EVERY PARCEL'S ADDRESS ON EARTH, AND GIVE
-- THE WORLD A PLANET DIMENSION.
--
-- Since P4 a parcel's real 0.5° Earth cell has been DERIVED — `parcelCell(site_id,
-- x, y)` (packages/shared/src/parcelGrid.ts), a pure function of two integers that
-- were already frozen. That was safe only while nothing outside the process
-- depended on the answer. The owner then decided THE COUNTRY GOES ON THE DEED
-- (2026-08-10 Q3), and a deed is a document published on a chain that cannot be
-- recalled — while the derivation reads the generated Earth mask + country index,
-- which `pnpm --filter @shardkeep/scripts earth:mask` may legitimately rebuild. A
-- build step must never be able to move land a minted deed already names, so the
-- answer is written down here, once, and P6 reads THIS COLUMN and never the mask.
--
-- FOUR STATEMENTS RATHER THAN ONE, the shape 0044 and 0052 both use: drizzle's
-- single `ADD COLUMN … NOT NULL` cannot succeed on a populated table, so it is add
-- nullable → backfill → assert nothing was missed → tighten (NOT NULL, then UNIQUE).
-- MIGRATION-FIRST DEPLOY (0052's recorded rule, R18): the column is NOT NULL with no
-- default and new code selects it unconditionally, so the migration ships before the
-- API. Old code against the migrated schema is safe — it simply never mentions
-- earth_cell, and `valleys_site_id_uq` deliberately KEEPS ITS NAME below so
-- `isSiteIdCollision` (game/world.ts) still recognises a raced site allocation
-- during that window.
--
-- WHY THE ADDRESS BOOK IS EMBEDDED HERE AS DATA. The mapping lives in TypeScript
-- (a Hilbert walk over a base64 land mask, then a country vote), and `db:migrate`
-- is `drizzle-kit migrate` — a pure-SQL runner with no way to call into it. A
-- PL/pgSQL re-implementation of the walk would be a SECOND planet, which is the one
-- bug lib/globeGeo.ts exists to prevent. So the migration carries the RESULT
-- instead: the whole of claimable Earth as 120 patches of 491 cells, in exactly the
-- order `patchCells` streams them. It is a frozen snapshot of one planet, not a
-- second opinion about it, and apps/api/test/earth-address.test.ts re-derives all
-- 58,920 numbers from `parcelCell` and fails if a single one differs — which is
-- also R15's freeze: regenerating the Earth is now a migration, not a build step.
--
-- MEASURED (2026-08-10, real code + the checked-in 0.5° mask/index): 61,903
-- claimable cells; the coarse-anchored stream reaches 59,071 of them (95.43%);
-- 120 patches × 491 = 58,920 parcels, 151 cells leftover. 58,920 distinct cells,
-- 0 duplicates, 0 countryless. Every existing parcel keeps the cell it already
-- resolved to — this migration writes down what the code already believed, exactly
-- as 0052 did for site_id.
--
-- The valley grid is NOT re-derived either: `fill_index` below reproduces
-- `plotFillOrder`'s 5→3→2→1 stride and `campCoords`'s 9-tile camp lattice in SQL,
-- and the numbers 25 / 20 / 9 are hardcoded ON PURPOSE — a migration is a frozen
-- historical record of the world as it was, so it must not follow constants.ts if
-- those ever change. The same test asserts this SQL and the live functions agree.
ALTER TABLE "plots" ADD COLUMN "earth_cell" integer;--> statement-breakpoint
-- THE BACKFILL. `book` is the frozen stream (site_id, index-in-patch → cell);
-- `fill` is the valley's 491 deeded tiles in plot-assignment order, camps excluded.
-- A plot on a camp tile, or in a valley past the last patch, matches NOTHING here
-- and is caught loudly by the next statement rather than silently addressed.
-- Every existing valley is Earth by definition: planet_id does not exist yet.
